#====================================================================
# DRAFT-gcem-1.16.0-ohos-libm-long-double-boundary
# 触发事件: gcem 1.16.0 HarmonyOS PC 适配（R02 上游测试）
#          上游 log/log10/log2 三个入口在 1e-500L 用例 SIGABRT
#====================================================================

id:                     DRAFT-gcem-1.16.0-ohos-libm-long-double-boundary
trigger_tool:           gcem|clang|libm|test|log|log10|log2
phase:                  testing|verification|porting
error_signature: 'std::log\(1e-500L\).* -inf|log\(1e-500\).*Expected value: -inf|Actual value: -1151|libc\+\+abi: terminating due to uncaught exception.*test fail|gcem::log.*SIGABRT|gcem::log\(1e-500\)'

severity:               3
category:               ohos|libm|long-double|reference-oracle|upstream-test|underflow
match_keywords:         OHOS, long double, libm, std::log, reference oracle,
                         underflow, 1e-500, -inf, finite, gcem, clang-15,
                         aarch64, SIGABRT, upstream test, 平台边界

title: 'OHOS long double libm reference boundary: std::log(1e-500L)=-inf vs library finite value'

root_cause: >
  On OHOS aarch64 with the clang-15 toolchain, long double is IEEE-754 quad (sizeof 16,
  LDBL_MIN 0x1p-16382), so 1e-500L is a perfectly normal, representable value (about
  0x1.0675b44007b734ff1d9a8873b40ap-1661). The platform reference implementation
  std::log(long double) returns -inf for such a normal quad value (probe: std::log(0x1p-1600L)
  = -inf, while std::log(2.0L)=0.693147 and std::log(1e5L)=11.5129 work), i.e. the OHOS
  libm long-double path mis-reports underflow/infinity in the extreme-small normal range.
  A constexpr math library such as gcem computing log by its own continued-fraction /
  mantissa-expansion returns the mathematically correct finite value (-1151.29 for 1e-500).
  Upstream tests that compare library result against the platform reference oracle then
  fail (expected -inf, actual -1151.29) and abort via std::runtime_error (SIGABRT).
  The mismatch is therefore a platform libm boundary behavior, not a defect of the library.

affected_scope: >
  Any OHOS aarch64/clang-15 adaptation whose upstream test suite compares a constexpr or
  self-implemented math result against std::/libm long-double reference values in the
  extreme-small normal range (roughly < 1e-300 in long double). Readily hit by header-only
  compile-time math libraries (gcem, and similar constexpr math code) on their log/log10/
  log2/exp-family boundary tests.

verification_cmd: |
  LD_PROBE=/storage/Users/currentUser/tmp/ld_probe
  /data/service/hnp/bin/clang++ -std=c++17 -xc++ - -o "$LD_PROBE" <<'EOF'
  #include <cstdio>
  #include <cmath>
  int main(){ long double x = 1e-500L;
    std::printf("sizeof(long double)=%zu\n", sizeof(long double));
    std::printf("x=%La std::log(x)=%Lg gcem_expected≈-1151.29\n", x, std::log(x));
    std::printf("std::log(0x1p-1600L)=%Lg\n", std::log(0x1p-1600L)); return 0; }
  EOF
  "$LD_PROBE"

rec_priority:           2
rec_approach:           reference-oracle-boundary-separation
rec_description: >
  Do NOT downgrade the upstream assertion, delete the case, or fake a pass, and do NOT claim
  the library is fixed when the platform libm is the abnormal side. Before judging a
  library-vs-reference math mismatch in the extreme-small long double domain, validate the
  reference oracle itself:
    1) Confirm the type semantics: sizeof(long double) and LDBL_MIN on the target
       (OHOS: quad, 0x1p-16382 — so 1e-500L is normal, not subnormal).
    2) Probe the reference implementation at the failing point, e.g. std::log(0x1p-1600L)
       == -inf, plus nearby finite controls std::log(2.0L)/std::log(1e5L).
    3) Verify the library value is mathematically finite and correct independently
       (for gcem: -1151.29 = ln(1e-500)).
    4) If the reference is abnormal and the library value is correct, record the case as a
       platform libm long-double boundary record: keep the test entry runnable, document
       expected-vs-actual, attribute to OHOS libm, and do not block the library quality
       conclusion on it.
  Reuse this comparison strategy wherever a constexpr/self-implemented math library hits a
  std:: reference oracle mismatch in the extreme-small long double domain.

rec_command: |
  clang++ -std=c++17 -O2 -I<lib>/include <test>.cpp -o <test>.test
  && <test>.test;
  LD_PROBE=/storage/Users/currentUser/tmp/ld_probe
  /data/service/hnp/bin/clang++ -std=c++17 -xc++ - -o "$LD_PROBE" <<'EOF'
  #include <cstdio>
  #include <cmath>
  int main(){ printf("sz=%zu log(1e-500L)=%Lg\n", sizeof(long double), std::log(1e-500L)); }
  EOF
  && "$LD_PROBE"

rec_auto_fix:           false
status:                 draft

validation: >
  gcem 1.16.0 R02 on OHOS aarch64 clang-15: upstream tests/ 43 entries compiled and run;
  40 passed; log, log10, log2 fail at gcem::log(1e-500L) vs std::log(1e-500L) with
  "Expected value: -inf / Actual value: -1151.29" then std::runtime_error (SIGABRT).
  Probe confirmed sizeof(long double)=16, 1e-500L normal (0x1.0675b44007b734ff1d9a8873b40ap-1661),
  std::log(0x1p-1600L)=-inf while std::log(2.0L) and std::log(1e5L) are correct, and gcem's
  -1151.29 equals ln(1e-500) mathematically. Original run log sha256:
  e1ddfc022fcb065d623489f60fb6bd338b63f68f16cee54314ec04e1a35202e8.
  Conclusion: platform libm long-double boundary; gcem behavior is correct; no source change
  to gcem made; ledger archived at archives/g/gcem/1.16.0/reports/upstream-test-ledger.md.